# 0036 XeGfxAccel: hardware colour, late-frame GPU clock floor, numbered surface marks (no stale frames)
#
# Base:  kexts/NVRM/accel after 0002 + 0009 + 0012 + 0024 + 0029
# Files: kexts/NVRM/accel/nvrm-accel.cpp, kexts/NVRM/accel/xegfx_accel_names.h
# Apply: patch -p1 < patches/0036-accel-colour-gt-floor-numbered-marks.patch
# Build: XeGfxAccel 4ec0bc233300 (build/nvgfx/build_nvaccel.sh xegfxaccel; needs core 0035, plugin 0037)
#
# Hardware colour:
#   - performTransaction reads the transaction's colour state (IOAccelDisplayPipeTransaction2: pre / post gamma tables
#     through IOAccelDisplayPipeGammaTable2::getTableEntry, the 48-byte colour matrix); a table object or matrix that
#     changed is read again.  The flip worker hands a new state to the core ("xgPipeColour") before the frame's arming
#     write.  A state identical to the last one committed is skipped (CoreDisplay resends the same tables as new
#     objects about once a second).
#   - on by default with no rate limit: debug.xegfxaccel_iop_colour (boot argument xegfxcolour=0 starts it off),
#     iop_colour_ms (0), iop_colour_mtx (matrix layout); counters iop_colour_tx / _changes / _applied / _same / _fail,
#     iop_colour_last_us / _max_us.  An earlier 1 s limit skipped the slider's in-between states: the colour jumped
#     late in each direction and looked like hysteresis.
#
# Late-frame GPU clock floor: a deferred flip whose composite is not finished 3 ms after the worker took it (it will
# miss its vblank) raises the SLPC minimum to the maximum ("xgGtMin"); it goes back 150 ms after the last late frame.
# Linux's display does the same for a flip whose fence misses the vblank; i915's own waitboost never fires here (the
# flip waits for the plugin's mark, and the composite has always started).  Without it SLPC stays at RPn (550 MHz)
# for long stretches.  debug.xegfxaccel_iop_xe_boost_us (3000, 0 = off), _boost_hold_ms (150), _boost_mhz (0 = max);
# counters iop_xe_late, _boosts, _boost_floor.
#
# Numbered surface marks (the "animation plays again from the middle" bug):
#   - the plugin kept one pending bit per surface, released when the last command buffer writing it finished, and the
#     flip waited for that bit.  WindowServer's command buffers on a scanout surface overlap, so the bit could stay set
#     for 200+ ms with every command buffer short; the flip waited for work committed after its own transaction, ran
#     into its timeout and scanned the surface unfinished -- which still held a frame from two or three frames back.
#   - DRM client selector 13, WindowServer only: (surface, op, flags, seq) -> (selector 2's answer, seq).  op 1 makes
#     a new mark and returns its number, op 0 releases that number (0: every mark of the surface), op 2 records the
#     layout only.  The table keeps one mark per command buffer (64 entries).  A transaction records the newest mark
#     number when it is queued, and its flip waits only for marks up to it.  Selectors 1/2 keep the old meaning.
#   - the flip waits up to debug.xegfxaccel_iop_xe_mark_ms (200, was a fixed 100) before arming anyway; a mark it gave
#     up on is logged when it clears (iop_xe_late_cleared).
